s infrastructure [Data: Entities (116, 120, 118); Relationships (160)]. This localized approach ensures efficient cable management and direct access to network components for each floor, optimizing performance and simplifying maintenance compared to a single, centrally located room serving multiple levels.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Entrance Spaces as Critical Points for Service Entry and Protection\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Entrance spaces are identified as critical points within the building infrastructure where telecommunication services, such as fiber optic and copper cables, enter the building. The standard 569 II includes specific requirements for these areas, emphasizing the need for protection and accessibility [Data: Entities (117, 123); Relationships (153, 166)]. The integration of these entrance spaces with other telecommunication components, like work areas and telecommunication closets, is crucial for establishing a robust and well-managed network infrastructure from the point of service entry throughout the building.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Structured Cabling Management and Distance Limitations in Telecommunication Closets\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Telecommunication closets, whether general or per floor, are designed for housing telecommunications equipment and managing network cabling. A key practical specification for these closets is the maximum horizontal cabling distance, limited to 90 meters. This limitation is crucial for ensuring efficient signal transmission and effective network infrastructure management within a building [Data: Entities (118); Relationships (157)]. The relationship between telecommunication closets and building entrances further emphasizes the need for careful planning of cabling pathways from the point of service entry to the end-user locations.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 6.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is mode